      "content": {
        "raw_text": "\n--- Page 455 ---\n420\nRESISTANCE AND CONDUCTIVITY.\n[300.\nof the electromotive intensity. We thus obtain the following ex-\npressions for $W$, the quantity of work expended in unit of time:\n$$\\begin{aligned}\nW & =X u+Y v+Z w ; \\\\\n& =R_{1} u^{2}+R_{2} v^{2}+R_{3} w^{2}+\\left(P_{1}+Q_{1}\\right) v w+\\left(P_{2}+Q_{2}\\right) w u+\\left(P_{3}+Q_{3}\\right) u v ; \\\\\n& =r_{1} X^{2}+r_{2} Y^{2}+r_{3} Z^{2}+\\left(p_{1}+q_{1}\\right) Y Z+\\left(p_{2}+q_{2}\\right) Z X+\\left(p_{3}+q_{3}\\right) X Y .\n\\end{aligned}$$\n(8)\n(9)\n(10)\nBy a proper choice of axes, (9) may be deprived of the terms\ninvolving the products of $u, v, w$ or else (10) of those involving\nthe products of $X, Y, Z$. The system of axes, however, which\nreduces $W$ to the form\n$$R_{1} u^{2}+R_{2} v^{2}+R_{3} w^{2}$$\nis not in general the same as that which reduces it to the form\n$$r_{1} X^{2}+r_{2} Y^{2}+r_{3} Z^{2}$$\nIt is only when the coefficients $P_{1}, P_{2}, P_{3}$ are equal respectively\nto $Q_{1}, Q_{2}, Q_{3}$ that the two systems of axes coincide.\nIf with Thomson * we write\nand\n$$\\left.\\begin{array}{rlr}\nP=S+T, & Q=S-T ; \\\\\np=s+t, & q=s-t ;\n\\end{array}\\right\\}$$\n(11)\nthen we have\n$$\\left.\\begin{array}{l}\n{[P Q R]=R_{1} R_{2} R_{3}+2 S_{1} S_{2} S_{3}-S_{1}^{2} R_{1}-S_{2}^{2} R_{2}-S_{3}^{2} R_{3}} \\\\\n\\quad+2\\left(S_{1} T_{2} T_{3}+S_{2} T_{3} T_{1}+S_{3} T_{1} T_{2}\\right)+R_{1} T_{1}^{2}+R_{2} T_{2}^{2}+R_{3} T_{3}^{2}\n\\end{array}\\right\\}$$\n(12)\nand\n$$\\left.\\begin{array}{l}\n{[P Q R] r_{1}=R_{2} R_{3}-S_{1}^{2}+T_{1}^{2},} \\\\\n{[P Q R] s_{1}=T_{2} T_{3}+S_{2} S_{3}-R_{1} S_{1},} \\\\\n{[P Q R] t_{1}=R_{1} T_{1}+S_{2} T_{3}+S_{3} T_{2} .}\n\\end{array}\\right\\}$$\n(13)\nIf therefore we cause $S_{1}, S_{2}, S_{3}$ to disappear, the coefficients 8\nwill not also disappear unless the coefficients $T$ are zero.\n\nCondition of Stability.\n300.] Since the equilibrium of electricity is stable, the work\nspent in maintaining the current must always be positive. The\nconditions that $W$ must be positive are that the three coefficients\n$R_{1}, R_{2}, R_{3}$, and the three expressions\n$$\\left.\\begin{array}{l}\n4 R_{2} R_{3}-\\left(P_{1}+Q_{1}\\right)^{2}, \\\\\n4 R_{3} R_{1}-\\left(P_{2}+Q_{2}\\right)^{2}, \\\\\n4 R_{1} R_{2}-\\left(F_{3}+Q_{3}\\right)^{2},\n\\end{array}\\right\\}$$\n(14)\nmust all be positive.\nThere are similar conditions for the coefficients of conductivity.\n\n* Trans. R. S. Edin., 1853-4, p. 165.\n",
        "mathpix_markdown": "\n--- Page 455 ---\n\nof the electromotive intensity. We thus obtain the following ex\npressions for $W$, the quantity of work expended in unit of time:\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\nW & =X u+Y v+Z w ;  \\tag{8}\\\\\n& =R_{1} u^{2}+R_{2} v^{2}+R_{3} w^{2}+\\left(P_{1}+Q_{1}\\right) v w+\\left(P_{2}+Q_{2}\\right) w u+\\left(P_{3}+Q_{3}\\right) u v ;  \\tag{9}\\\\\n& =r_{1} X^{2}+r_{2} Y^{2}+r_{3} Z^{2}+\\left(p_{1}+q_{1}\\right) Y Z+\\left(p_{2}+q_{2}\\right) Z X+\\left(p_{3}+q_{3}\\right) X Y . \\tag{10}\n\\end{align*}$$\n\n\nBy a proper choice of axes, (9) may be deprived of the terms\n involving the products of $u, v, w$ or else (10) of those involving\n the products of $X, Y, Z$. The system of axes, however, which\n reduces $W$ to the form\n\n$$R_{1} u^{2}+R_{2} v^{2}+R_{3} w^{2}$$\n\nis not in general the same as that which reduces it to the form\n\n$$r_{1} X^{2}+r_{2} Y^{2}+r_{3} Z^{2}$$\n\n\nIt is only when the coefficients $P_{1}, P_{2}, P_{3}$ are equal respectively\n to $Q_{1}, Q_{2}, Q_{3}$ that the two systems of axes coincide.\n\n\nIf with Thomson * we write\n\nand\n\n$$\\left.\\begin{array}{rlr}\nP=S+T, & Q=S-T ;  \\tag{11}\\\\\np=s+t, & q=s-t ;\n\\end{array}\\right\\}$$\n\nthen we have\n\n$$\\left.\\begin{array}{l}\n{[P Q R]=R_{1} R_{2} R_{3}+2 S_{1} S_{2} S_{3}-S_{1}^{2} R_{1}-S_{2}^{2} R_{2}-S_{3}^{2} R_{3}}  \\tag{12}\\\\\n\\quad+2\\left(S_{1} T_{2} T_{3}+S_{2} T_{3} T_{1}+S_{3} T_{1} T_{2}\\right)+R_{1} T_{1}^{2}+R_{2} T_{2}^{2}+R_{3} T_{3}^{2}\n\\end{array}\\right\\}$$\n\nand\n\n$$\\left.\\begin{array}{l}\n{[P Q R] r_{1}=R_{2} R_{3}-S_{1}^{2}+T_{1}^{2},}  \\tag{13}\\\\\n{[P Q R] s_{1}=T_{2} T_{3}+S_{2} S_{3}-R_{1} S_{1},} \\\\\n{[P Q R] t_{1}=R_{1} T_{1}+S_{2} T_{3}+S_{3} T_{2} .}\n\\end{array}\\right\\}$$\n\n\nIf therefore we cause $S_{1}, S_{2}, S_{3}$ to disappear, the coefficients 8\n will not also disappear unless the coefficients $T$ are zero.\n\n\n\\section*{Condition of Stability.}\n\n300.] Since the equilibrium of electricity is stable, the work\n spent in maintaining the current must always be positive. The\n conditions that $W$ must be positive are that the three coefficients\n $R_{1}, R_{2}, R_{3}$, and the three expressions\n\n$$\\left.\\begin{array}{l}\n4 R_{2} R_{3}-\\left(P_{1}+Q_{1}\\right)^{2},  \\tag{14}\\\\\n4 R_{3} R_{1}-\\left(P_{2}+Q_{2}\\right)^{2}, \\\\\n4 R_{1} R_{2}-\\left(F_{3}+Q_{3}\\right)^{2},\n\\end{array}\\right\\}$$\n\nmust all be positive.\n\nThere are similar conditions for the coefficients of conductivity.\n\n\n\\footnotetext{\n* Trans. R. S. Edin., 1853-4, p. 165.\n}\n",
